#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152948} :activity/id #uuid "bb4b44ed-5cd5-537a-880d-adb3d446a517" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050697 :topic/id #uuid "5f607ed3-b581-5e92-9101-c32dd179ab13" :topic/math-academy-id 2792} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152948 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153431 :tutorial/content "We can apply quantifiers to predicates with more than one variable.\n\nFor example, the statement\n\n$$\\forall m \\in \\mathbb{N}, \\, \\exists n \\in \\mathbb{Z}, \\, n > m$$\n\nreads as follows:\n\n$\\qquad$ *For all $m \\in \\mathbb{N},$ there exists $n \\in \\mathbb{Z}$ such that $n > m.$*\n\nThis statement is true. Informally, it states that given *any* natural number ${\\color{blue}{m}},$ there is an integer ${\\color{red}{n}}$ that's larger than ${\\color{blue}{m}}.$\n\n- If ${\\color{blue}{m}}={\\color{blue}{1}}\\in \\mathbb N$ and we select ${\\color{red}{n}}={\\color{red}{2}}\\in \\mathbb Z,$ we have ${\\color{red}{2}} > {\\color{blue}{1}}$ which is true. $\\quad{\\color{green}{\\checkmark}}$\n- If ${\\color{blue}{m}}={\\color{blue}{2}}\\in \\mathbb N$ and we select ${\\color{red}{n}}={\\color{red}{3}}\\in \\mathbb Z,$ we have ${\\color{red}{3}} > {\\color{blue}{2}}$ which is true. $\\quad{\\color{green}{\\checkmark}}$\n- If ${\\color{blue}{m}}={\\color{blue}{3}}\\in \\mathbb N$ and we select ${\\color{red}{n}}={\\color{red}{4}}\\in \\mathbb Z,$ we have ${\\color{red}{4}} > {\\color{blue}{3}}$ which is true. $\\quad{\\color{green}{\\checkmark}}$\n\nAnd so on. It's easy to see that this statement is true since for any $m\\in\\mathbb N,$ we can simply select $n = m+1,$ and thus $n > m.$\n\n**Watch out!** The order in which the quantifiers are stated is very important. If we swap the quantifiers in this example, we get $$\\exists n \\in \\mathbb{Z}, \\, \\forall m \\in \\mathbb{N}, \\, n > m$$ which reads as follows:\n\n$\\qquad$ *There exists $n \\in \\mathbb{Z}$ such that for all $m \\in \\mathbb{N},$ $n > m.$*\n\nThis new statement has an entirely different meaning! Informally, it states that there is an integer $n$ that's larger than *every* natural number, which is false!\n\nHowever, quantifiers *of the same type* can be swapped. For example,\n\n$$\\exists m \\in \\mathbb{N}, \\, \\exists n \\in \\mathbb{Z}, \\, n > m \\: \\equiv \\: \\exists n \\in \\mathbb{Z}, \\, \\exists m \\in \\mathbb{N}, \\, n > m.$$\n\nFinally, if we have two or more quantifiers of the same type defined over the same domain, we can write only one quantifier followed by the corresponding variables.\n\nFor example, the (false) statement $$\\forall x \\in \\mathbb{R}, \\, \\forall y \\in \\mathbb{R}, \\, x \\leq y$$ can be written more compactly as $$\\forall \\, x,y \\in \\mathbb{R}, \\, x \\leq y.$$\n\nSimilarly, the (true) statement $$\\begin{aligned}∃x\\in R,\\,∃y\\in R,\\,x\\le y\\end{aligned}$$ can be written as $$\\exists \\, x,y \\in \\mathbb{R}, \\, x \\leq y.$$\n\nWe'll learn more about determining the truth value of a statement containing multiple quantifiers later in the lesson." :tutorial/id #uuid "992d8409-4fd0-588b-8307-afdd3dcaae09" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7942 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "df03f699-9bf6-5301-9f32-5a1b82728cef" :step/math-academy-id 145948 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152949}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152949 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152093 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151817 :question/id #uuid "aca83a15-ecdd-50ad-b384-b27d1f534497" :question/math-academy-id "e-12746"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d463a0dd-784d-55b6-a598-60819f5330d7" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050884}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152453} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152454} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186381467} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186381804} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186381845} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401790} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401797}] :knowledge-point/title "Translating Statements Involving Nested Quantifiers"} :step/id #uuid "7a975b0a-46f1-5b5b-b4d1-80613a1bc7ac" :step/math-academy-id 145949 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152950}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152950 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153437 :tutorial/content "Consider the following quantified expression\n\n$$\\exists y \\in \\mathbb{N} \\big( \\, x > 0 \\Rightarrow \\forall x \\in \\mathbb{R}, y < 10 \\, \\big)$$\n\nThis expression has two quantifiers. The **scope** of each quantifier is the range in the expression where the quantifier is applied. We can use parentheses to avoid ambiguity in complex expressions.\n\nIn this example, the scope of the quantifier $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\exists y}$ is the following:\n\n$$\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\exists y} \\in \\mathbb{N} \\big( \\, \\overbrace{x > 0 \\Rightarrow \\forall x \\in \\mathbb{R}, y < 10}^{\\textrm{scope of ∃y}} \\, \\big),$$\n\nand the scope of $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\forall x}$ is the following:\n\n$$\\exists y \\in \\mathbb{N} \\big( \\, x > 0 \\Rightarrow \\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\forall x} \\in \\mathbb{R}, \\overbrace{y < 10}^{\\textrm{scope of ∀x}} \\, \\big).$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "7034115f-f0c7-508f-8cdd-fd5c4a8380f7" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7998 :tutorial/title "The Scope of a Quantifier"} :step/id #uuid "ac12610e-248a-5d1f-9e23-576263a1e1c9" :step/math-academy-id 145953 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152951}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152951 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153449 :tutorial/content "The occurrence of a variable is called **bound** if it is placed right next to a quantifier or lies within the scope of a quantifier that uses the same variable. Otherwise, the occurrence is called **free**.\n\nFor example, let's examine the occurrences of variables in the following statement:\n\n$$\\exists y \\in \\mathbb{N} \\big( \\, x > 0 \\Rightarrow \\forall x \\in \\mathbb{R}, y < 10 \\, \\big).$$\n\n- The following occurrence of $y$ is *bound* since it is placed right next to the quantifier $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\exists}{:}$ $$\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\exists} \\!\\! \\underset{\\substack{\\phantom{.} \\[4pt] \\uparrow \\[6pt] \\large\\phantom{.}\\bbox[2pt,Moccasin]{\\text{b}}}}{y} \\!\\! \\in \\mathbb{N} \\big( \\, x > 0 \\Rightarrow \\forall x \\in \\mathbb{R}, y < 10 \\, \\big)$$\n- The following occurrence of $x$ is *free* since it isn't placed right next to a quantifier and it doesn't lie within the scope of a quantifier that uses $x{:}$ $$\\exists y \\in \\mathbb{N} \\big( \\, \\underset{\\substack{\\phantom{.} \\[4pt] \\uparrow \\[6pt] \\large\\phantom{.}\\bbox[2pt,LightBlue]{\\text{f}}}}{x} \\!\\! > 0 \\Rightarrow \\forall x \\in \\mathbb{R}, y < 10 \\, \\big)$$\n- The following occurrence of $x$ is *bound* since it is placed right next to the quantifier $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\forall}{:}$ $$\\exists y \\in \\mathbb{N} \\big( \\, x > 0 \\Rightarrow \\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\forall} \\!\\! \\underset{\\substack{\\phantom{.} \\[4pt] \\uparrow \\[6pt] \\large\\phantom{.}\\bbox[2pt,Moccasin]{\\text{b}}}}{x} \\!\\! \\in \\mathbb{R}, y < 10 \\, \\big)$$\n- The following occurrence of $y$ is *bound* since it lies within the scope of the quantifier $\\exists y$ that uses $y{:}$ $$\\exists y \\in \\mathbb{N} \\big( \\, \\overbrace{x > 0 \\Rightarrow \\forall x \\in \\mathbb{R}, \\!\\! \\underset{\\substack{\\phantom{.} \\[4pt] \\uparrow \\[6pt] \\large\\phantom{.}\\bbox[2pt,Moccasin]{\\text{b}}}}{y} \\!\\! < 10}^{\\textrm{scope of ∃y}} \\, \\big)$$\n\nSo, in our expression, we have:\n\n- two occurrences of $y,$ both of which are bound, and\n- two occurrences of $x,$ where one is free, and another is bound.\n\n$$\\exists \\bbox[2pt,Moccasin]{y} \\in \\mathbb{N} \\big( \\, \\bbox[2pt,LightBlue]{x} > 0 \\Rightarrow \\big( \\, \\forall \\bbox[2pt,Moccasin]{x} \\in \\mathbb{R}, \\bbox[2pt,Moccasin]{y} < 10 \\, \\big) \\, \\big)$$\n\nDistinguishing between free and bound occurrences is important since we are allowed to substitute values into free occurrences of variables. In particular, since the first occurrence of $\\bbox[2pt,LightBlue]{x}$ is free, we can substitute concrete values for this variable (but not for the other occurrence of $\\bbox[2pt,Moccasin]{x}$).\n\nTherefore, the above expression can be viewed as a predicate of one variable ($\\bbox[2pt,LightBlue]{x}$) since all occurrences of $y$ are bound." :tutorial/id #uuid "d98c614c-0abc-540c-a882-40069bc979d8" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8313 :tutorial/title "Free and Bound Occurrences of Variables"} :step/id #uuid "e67bda2d-11a8-533c-9ec8-a842ae2c208f" :step/math-academy-id 145954 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152952}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152952 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152094 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151819 :question/id #uuid "eb54c96d-6dd5-5edd-b6a6-460d18645a72" :question/math-academy-id "e-12748"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "051e28ef-ac7f-5b29-bd93-c1e24a4bc699" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152456} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152457} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186381832} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186381873} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186381880} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186390211} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186390218} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407163} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407165}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Free and Bound Occurrences of Variables"} :step/id #uuid "349da444-94cc-5fbc-9a91-cc3cf599c78d" :step/math-academy-id 145955 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152953}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152953 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153436 :tutorial/content "Consider the statement\n\n$$\\forall\\, m \\in \\mathbb{N}, \\, \\forall\\, n \\in \\mathbb{N}, \\, m > n$$\n\nthat reads as follows:\n\n$\\qquad$ For all natural $m$ and $n,$ we have $m > n.$\n\nIs the statement true or false? To figure this out, we proceed as described below.\n\nFirst, let's write down the inner predicate and determine its truth set: $$P(m): \\quad \\forall\\, n \\in \\mathbb{N}, \\, m > n.$$ Notice that this is a predicate in the variable $m$ since $m$ is a free variable in the $P(m)$ statement.\n\nThe predicate $P(m)$ is false for all values $m \\in \\mathbb {N}.$ For example, $$P(1) : \\quad \\forall\\, n \\in \\mathbb{N}, \\, 1 > n$$ is false since we can take $n=1.$ Similarly, $$P(2): \\quad \\forall\\, n \\in \\mathbb{N}, \\, 2 > n$$ is false, since we can take $n=2.$ In general, it's sufficient to take $n=m$ for any value of $m.$ As a result, the truth set of $P(m)$ is $T_P=\\emptyset.$\n\nNow, notice that we can write our original statement as follows: $$\\forall\\, m \\in \\mathbb{N}, \\, \\forall\\, n \\in \\mathbb{N}, \\, m > n \\: \\equiv \\: \\forall m \\in \\mathbb{N}, \\, P(m).$$ This statement is true whenever the *inner* predicate $P(m)$ is true over the universal set $\\mathbb N.$ However, this is not the case, as we discovered above.\n\nTherefore, the statement $\\forall\\, m \\in \\mathbb{N}, \\, \\forall\\, n \\in \\mathbb{N}, \\, m > n$ is false." :tutorial/id #uuid "860b857c-2cd9-531e-8868-db58f1c6a835" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7997 :tutorial/title "The Truth Value of a Statement Involving Nested Quantifiers"} :step/id #uuid "893b05d5-81e8-5350-beac-4c5747ea81d9" :step/math-academy-id 145958 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152954}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152954 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152095 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151818 :question/id #uuid "6434be6e-ad3f-572b-b64d-ead988cc2ca4" :question/math-academy-id "e-12747"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6927f03c-aa7a-56ac-8cd0-c036a11aadc9" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152455} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152476} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186381460} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186381797} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186381811} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186381818} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407164}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Truth Value of a Statement Involving Nested Quantifiers"} :step/id #uuid "913d2921-e79c-586a-9141-9bfd4b5e7097" :step/math-academy-id 145959}] :activity/title "Nested Quantifiers" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186152382}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046679} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407167} :activity/id #uuid "b9cfd82e-7160-5eb5-9330-87cea8b48cc4" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050697 :topic/id #uuid "5f607ed3-b581-5e92-9101-c32dd179ab13" :topic/math-academy-id 2792} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407166 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407165 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1943552a-2f42-5c04-b2b2-141962e83629" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045500} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407145} {:answer/id #uuid "2362167e-158f-5ff6-98a3-0b706701da84" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045500} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407147} {:answer/id #uuid "321d0d48-cf92-590c-b6d4-e7a9c6d3ddbf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045500} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407149} {:answer/id #uuid "bfee88a5-cc20-5302-a89d-798128cc26a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045500} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407155} {:answer/id #uuid "f6c05a0d-3046-5d14-b8a4-1703eef736d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045500} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407158}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407147} :answer-field/id #uuid "d3f68dc1-1788-54d0-8ac8-3e61f521a037" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407162}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "39d9d2c3-0d6e-5d14-b069-fbe9f014d46f" :question/math-academy-id "q-183674" :question/problem "$$\n(\\,∀{x}_{\\begin{aligned} \\\\ ↑ \\\\ \\text{I}\\end{aligned}}\\in R,\\,0<x<1\\,)⇒(\\,∃y\\in R,\\,{x}_{\\begin{aligned} \\\\ ↑ \\\\ \\text{II}\\end{aligned}}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡{y}_{\\begin{aligned} \\\\ ↑ \\\\ \\text{III}\\end{aligned}}\\,)\n$$\n\nWhich occurrences of the variables in the expression above are free?" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine our expression and mark the occurrences of the variables as bound $(\\text{b})$ or free $(\\text{f}).$\n\n- Occurrence I of the variable $x$ is *bound* since it is placed right next to the quantifier $∀:$\n\n$$\n(\\,∀{x}_{\\begin{aligned} \\\\ ↑ \\\\ \\text{b}\\end{aligned}}\\in R,\\,0<x<1\\,)⇒(\\,∃y\\in R,\\,x=\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,)\n$$\n\n- Occurrence II of the variable $x$ is *free* since it isn't placed right next to a quantifier and it doesn't lie within the scope of a quantifier that uses $x.$\n\n- Occurrence III of the variable $y$ is *bound* since it lies within the scope of the quantifier $∃y$ that uses $y:$\n\n$$\n(\\,∀x\\in R,\\,0<x<1\\,)⇒(\\,∃y\\in R,\\,\\overset{\\text{scope of }∃y}{\\overset{⏞}{x=\\operatorname{sin}⁡{y}_{\\begin{aligned} \\\\ ↑ \\\\ \\text{b}\\end{aligned}}}})\n$$\n\nThe occurrences of variables in the expression are as follows:\n\n$$\n(\\,∀{x}_{\\begin{aligned} \\\\ ↑ \\\\ \\text{b}\\end{aligned}}\\in R,\\,0<{x}_{\\begin{aligned} \\\\ ↑ \\\\ \\text{b}\\end{aligned}}<1\\,)⇒(\\,∃{y}_{\\begin{aligned} \\\\ ↑ \\\\ \\text{b}\\end{aligned}}\\in R,\\,{x}_{\\begin{aligned} \\\\ ↑ \\\\ \\text{f}\\end{aligned}}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡{y}_{\\begin{aligned} \\\\ ↑ \\\\ \\text{b}\\end{aligned}}\\,)\n$$\n\nTherefore, the free occurrence is \"II only.\""} :step/id #uuid "9f9fc0a2-320f-5251-a5a9-6da122c5a3b7" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407168}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407167 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407163 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2108dc3f-8bf7-5722-88d5-6553aea7bf61" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045500} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407146} {:answer/id #uuid "2ab77857-2df1-5afa-b593-651b139d8699" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045500} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407148} {:answer/id #uuid "a1c2ef64-ac61-56b4-a3f5-211311616d33" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045500} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407152} {:answer/id #uuid "b6153063-5e62-52e4-9bee-993b29720aee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045500} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407154} {:answer/id #uuid "fb316e3c-6f70-5a11-be3f-2488818c1837" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045500} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407159}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407148} :answer-field/id #uuid "68d7f5e7-3696-5415-aa0d-dffd949f398d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407160}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "085a10d3-7324-5921-95c5-eaa8f395984a" :question/math-academy-id "q-183620" :question/problem "$$\n∀{k}_{\\begin{aligned} \\\\ ↑ \\\\ \\text{I}\\end{aligned}}\\in (0,1),\\,{n}_{\\begin{aligned} \\\\ ↑ \\\\ \\text{II}\\end{aligned}}+{k}^{2}\\ge {k}_{\\begin{aligned} \\\\ ↑ \\\\ \\text{III}\\end{aligned}}\n$$\n\nWhich occurrences of the variables in the expression above are free?" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine our expression and mark the occurrences of the variables as bound $(\\text{b})$ or free $(\\text{f}).$\n\n- Occurrence I of the variable $k$ is *bound* since it's placed right next to the quantifier $∀:$\n\n$$\n∀{k}_{\\begin{aligned} \\\\ ↑ \\\\ \\text{b}\\end{aligned}}\\in (0,1),\\,n+{k}^{2}\\ge k\n$$\n\n- Occurrence II of the variable $n$ is *free* since it isn't placed right next to a quantifier and it doesn't lie within the scope of a quantifier that uses $n.$\n\n- Occurrence III of the variable $k$ is *bound* since it lies within the scope of the quantifier $∀k$ that uses $k:$\n\n$$\n∀k\\in (0,1),\\,\\overset{\\text{scope of }∀k}{\\overset{⏞}{n+{k}^{2}\\ge {k}_{\\begin{aligned} \\\\ ↑ \\\\ \\text{b}\\end{aligned}}}}\n$$\n\nThe occurrences of variables in the expression are as follows:\n\n$$\n∀\\,{k}_{\\begin{aligned} \\\\ ↑ \\\\ \\text{b}\\end{aligned}}\\in (0,1),\\,{n}_{\\begin{aligned} \\\\ ↑ \\\\ \\text{f}\\end{aligned}}+{k}^{2}\\ge {k}_{\\begin{aligned} \\\\ ↑ \\\\ \\text{b}\\end{aligned}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the free occurrence is \"II only.\""} :step/id #uuid "a8af4b36-6125-5654-ab7b-ed343d27d7c3" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407166}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407168 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407164 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "323a480f-fb01-5766-92ab-c2eb831689ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045500} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407150} {:answer/id #uuid "6c8dd577-5b41-5386-91e2-6d8dadf4331d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045500} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407151} {:answer/id #uuid "abae491e-3dfc-5abb-b6f5-dc7f6456e165" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045500} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407153} {:answer/id #uuid "ca49dde2-0454-5f89-bafe-f932d21ec499" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045500} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407156} {:answer/id #uuid "e4be5bf2-f7c6-557b-967a-fefc572c2364" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045500} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407157}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407157} :answer-field/id #uuid "d0f1732b-6bd9-5f1d-9035-c362872ddd7b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407161}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "0a806540-0634-5e11-9ba2-ff988650a653" :question/math-academy-id "q-183877" :question/problem "Let $p\\in Q$ and $q\\in Q.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $∃\\,p\\in Q,\\,2p=-1.$\n- The truth set of the predicate $(\\,∃\\,p\\in Q,\\,p\\cdot q=-1\\,)$ is $Q.$\n- $∃\\,q,p\\in Q,\\,p\\cdot q=-1.$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that the statement \n\n$$\n∃\\,q,p\\in Q,\\,p\\cdot q=-1\n$$\n\nfrom option III reads as follows:\n\n$\\,$ There exist rationals $q$ and $p,$ such that $p\\cdot q=-1.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true, $2p=-1$ for some rational number $p$ (e.g., for $p=-\\frac{1}{2}$).\n\n- Statement II is false. For the predicate\n\n$$\nP(q):\\,∃\\,p\\in Q,\\,p\\cdot q=-1\n$$\n\nwe note that $P(q)$ is true for some $q\\in Q$ and false for others. For example, \n\n$$\nP(0):\\,∃\\,p\\in Q,\\,p\\cdot 0=-1\n$$\n\nis false. However, \n\n$$\nP(1):\\,∃\\,p\\in Q,\\,p\\cdot 1=-1\n$$\n\nis true, since we can take $p=-1.$\n\n- Statement III is true. The sentence \n\n$$\n(\\,∃\\,q,p\\in Q,\\,p\\cdot q=-1\\,)\n$$\n\n \ncan be written as\n\n$$\n∃q\\in Q\\,(\\,{{∃p\\in Q,\\,p\\cdot q=-1}_{⏟}}_{P(q)}\\,)\n$$\n\nwhich is true whenever the inner predicate $P(q)$ has a nonempty truth set. And according to the discussion in statement II, this is the case.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""} :step/id #uuid "f49534a6-4055-5be7-b362-951a0ada2b45"}] :activity/title "Nested Quantifiers" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407144}]}
